    content: "\nScreening equities for Shariah compliance is a two-stage process: remove the businesses that are impermissible, then check the financial ratios of what remains. The second stage is the one most often skipped, and it is the one that changes most often.\n\n## Stage One: The Activity Screen\n\nA business whose primary activity is impermissible is excluded regardless of how profitable it is. The usual exclusions are interest-based financial services, alcohol, gambling, pork, and adult entertainment. The more difficult cases are the mixed businesses: a conglomerate with a permissible core and a small impermissible segment. Most screening standards tolerate a threshold of impermissible revenue rather than requiring zero, and the threshold is where the judgement sits.\n\n## Stage Two: The Financial Screen\n\nA business can be permissible in activity and still fail because of its balance sheet. The standard ratios are:\n\n- **Debt to assets.** Interest-bearing debt above a threshold relative to total assets.\n- **Cash and interest-bearing securities.** A business holding most of its assets in interest-bearing instruments.\n- **Receivables.** A high receivables ratio is sometimes screened, on the basis that trade credit is not the same as interest.\n\nThe thresholds differ between screening bodies, which is why two supposedly compliant lists can disagree.\n\n## Why the Financial Screen Changes\n\nA company that complied last quarter can fail this quarter because it took on debt, or because its cash balance grew, or because the index provider rebalanced the denominator. That is why a compliance list is a snapshot and why funds that track it must review continuously rather than annually.\n\n## Purification\n\nWhere a business earns a small amount of impermissible income, the screening body identifies a portion and removes it. In a fund, that amount is usually directed to charity. This is not a defect; it is the mechanism that keeps the portfolio compliant in practice.\n\n## Applying It Yourself\n\n- Start from a published compliant list if one is available for your market, rather than rebuilding the screen.\n- Check the date of the list and the screening body.\n- Apply your own activity judgement to the grey cases.\n- Note that compliance is not a recommendation: a compliant stock can be a poor investment.\n\n## The Index Question\n\nSome markets have a Shariah-compliant index that a fund tracks, which gives you the screen plus the diversification in one product. Where that exists, it is usually simpler than building the list yourself, and the review burden sits with the index provider.\n\nOur [Shariah screening guide](/article/psx-shariah-screening-kmi30-halal-investing) covers the equity-side application to the local market.\n"
